# `structure::truss`

Validates and compiles a pin-jointed truss.

## Signature

```cpp
structure::Truss structure::truss(const structure::Mesh& mesh, const structure::Bars& bars);
```

## What It Does

Checks that the mesh and bar properties describe a real truss and compiles them into an immutable `Truss`: it computes every bar's length, direction and axial stiffness `E A / L`, and keeps the connectivity for assembly. Use it once per structure, then solve the result as often as needed.

## Parameters

`mesh`
: Node coordinates and the `from` and `to` node of every bar. Two coordinates per node make a plane truss, three a space truss.

`bars`
: Area and modulus of every bar, each ending in the bar count.

## Returns

An immutable `structure::Truss`.

## Errors

Throws `std::invalid_argument` when coordinates are not real floating-point `[..., node, axis]` with two or three axes, a node index is out of range, a bar joins a node to itself, a bar has zero length, an area or modulus is not positive and finite, the per-bar lists do not end in the bar count, or the batch axes cannot broadcast.

## Interpretation and Use

Compilation does the validation once, so solving is free of it. A node that no bar touches is accepted here and rejected by `solve`, which finds a degree of freedom with no stiffness. Leading axes on the coordinates, areas or moduli build a batch of trusses with the same connectivity.

